  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-eee77039 Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-eee77039-web-logs-overview-cd2bcbfa9c32`) has six panels on the Kibana Sample Data Logs view:\n- Total requests (count)\n- Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n- Total bytes served (sum of `bytes`)\n- Requests over time (line, date histogram on `@timestamp`)\n- Responses by status code (bar, terms on `response.keyword`)\n- Top request countries (bar, terms on `geo.dest`)\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-eee77039 5xx spike\"** (id `aa42f29d-bbe2-4470-843d-52b0ab56eab0`) is an `.es-query` rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every 1m and counts documents where `response.keyword` is between 500 and 599 over the last 5 minutes. It fires when that count is greater than 10. It is enabled, and the alerting health check reports `ok`.\n\nChoices I made:\n- **Time range:** the dashboard uses the last year, since the sample data is static and may not fall in the last few minutes.\n- **5xx match:** I used a lexical range on `response.keyword`. This works because status codes are three digits, but it would need changing if codes ever had other formats.\n\nLimitations:\n- I did not check the sample data's actual values. The ES|QL endpoint and the console proxy both failed (404 and 401), so I worked from the field list alone.\n- Because the sample data is static, the rule will probably not fire unless 5xx responses arrive in the current 5-minute window. I did not confirm that the rule evaluates correctly.",
